// Private environment for the IntelliJ HTTP Client — the one file under `http/` that holds a secret.
// docs/DEVELOPMENT.md § IntelliJ HTTP Client explains the rest; the two things you need here are:
//
//   cp http/http-client.private.env.json.example http/http-client.private.env.json
//
// and that **the IDE finds the copy on its own while ijhttp does not** — without
// `--private-env-file` nothing defines the variable, so the request goes out carrying a literal
// `key={{google-maps-api-key}}` and Google refuses it with REQUEST_DENIED.
//
// The copy is gitignored and this template is tracked, so nothing real belongs below. This file is
// the only place the variable name appears in git — the public `http-client.env.json` holds hosts
// only, because a secret's name belongs with the secret.
//
// One key in one environment is enough: geocoding is a lookup you run while adding a venue, not
// something the deployed stack calls. Restrict it to the Geocoding API by IP, never by HTTP
// referrer.
//
// Comments here are an undocumented leniency of the client's parser, verified against ijhttp and
// read by `scripts/geocode-venues.py` too. If a future version ever rejects this file, delete them.
{
    "local": {
        "google-maps-api-key": ""
    },
    "staging": {
        "google-maps-api-key": ""
    },
    "production": {
        "google-maps-api-key": ""
    }
}
